+  <main>
+    <!-- ============================================================ -->
+    <section id="problem">
+      <h2>The problem</h2>
+      <p>
+        An AI shopping agent reads product pages written by strangers. Those
+        pages can lie, and they can carry instructions aimed at the model rather
+        than at the shopper. Give that agent a payment method and its worst day
+        becomes the merchant's chargeback and the shopper's problem.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        The common answer is to make the agent harder to fool: better prompts,
+        better guardrails, a more careful model. That is worth doing and it is
+        not sufficient. Resistance to manipulation is a property of one model on
+        one day. It is not something you can put in a contract, audit after the
+        fact, or point at when a customer disputes a charge.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        <strong>STEALTH takes the opposite position: do not trust the agent,
+        check it.</strong> The human signs a mandate that states what may be
+        bought and up to how much. Every checkout is measured against that
+        signed document by deterministic code. The agent's goodwill is not an
+        input.
+      </p>
+    </section>
