+    <!-- ============================================================ -->
+    <section id="pipeline">
+      <h2>The six stages</h2>
+      <p>
+        Stage order is a safety property, not a style choice. The cheapest and
+        most certain check runs first, so a forged credential is rejected for
+        the price of an elliptic-curve verification and never gets to spend a
+        model call, let alone reach a payment rail.
+      </p>
+
+      <div class="scroll">
+        <table>
+          <thead>
+            <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Kind</th><th>What it decides</th><th>Cost</th><th>On failure</th></tr>
+          </thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>1 Mandate verifier</strong><br><span class="id" style="font-size:12px">lib/mandate</span></td>
+              <td><span class="chip det">deterministic</span></td>
+              <td>Is the credential well-formed, signed by this agent's key, unexpired, and unspent?</td>
+              <td class="num">~134 µs</td>
+              <td><span class="chip decline">DECLINE</span></td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>2 Evidence extraction</strong><br><span class="id" style="font-size:12px">lib/extraction</span></td>
+              <td><span class="chip det">deterministic</span></td>
+              <td>Separates merchant assertions from untrusted listing text; runs a closed-taxonomy category scan and an injection-marker scan.</td>
+              <td class="num">sub-ms</td>
+              <td>n/a</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>3 Intent-cart diff</strong><br><span class="id" style="font-size:12px">lib/ai</span></td>
+              <td><span class="chip model">model call</span></td>
+              <td>Where does the cart depart from the mandate? Clause by clause, with severity and confidence. <strong>Evidence, not a decision.</strong></td>
+              <td class="num">seconds</td>
+              <td><span class="chip stepup">STEP_UP</span></td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>4 Threshold policy</strong><br><span class="id" style="font-size:12px">lib/policy</span></td>
+              <td><span class="chip det">deterministic</span></td>
+              <td>Turns the diff into a verdict against fixed thresholds. The only component that can allow a purchase.</td>
+              <td class="num">sub-ms</td>
+              <td>n/a</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>5 Human escalation</strong><br><span class="id" style="font-size:12px">lib/step-up</span></td>
+              <td><span class="chip stepup">human</span></td>
+              <td>Anything the policy will not decide alone waits for the principal's answer.</td>
+              <td>as long as it takes</td>
+              <td>stays pending</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>6 Audit chain</strong><br><span class="id" style="font-size:12px">lib/audit</span></td>
+              <td><span class="chip det">deterministic</span></td>
+              <td>Appends every step to a SHA-256 hash chain over canonical JSON.</td>
+              <td class="num">sub-ms</td>
+              <td>n/a</td>
+            </tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+
+      <h3>The separation that matters</h3>
+      <p>
+        Stage 3 produces an <code>AuthorizationDiff</code>: a set of clauses, each
+        naming one dimension (category, budget, quantity, injected instruction),
+        the authorized value, the attempted value, a severity and an
+        explanation. It carries a confidence score. It carries no verdict, and
+        the schema gives it no field in which to express one.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        Stage 4 reads that object and applies fixed thresholds. The same diff
+        always produces the same verdict. All of the variance in the system
+        lives in stage 3, and none of it can reach a payment without passing
+        through a rule that does not vary.
+      </p>
+    </section>
