+    <section id="trust">
+      <h2>Trust boundaries</h2>
+      <p>
+        Four parties, and STEALTH trusts exactly one of them.
+      </p>
+      <div class="scroll">
+        <table>
+          <thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>Trusted?</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>The principal</strong></td>
+              <td><span class="chip allow">trusted</span></td>
+              <td>Their Ed25519 signature over a canonical mandate payload is the root of authority. Nothing else can grant permission.</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>The agent</strong></td>
+              <td><span class="chip decline">untrusted</span></td>
+              <td>Software that may be hijacked. It can present a mandate and a cart; it cannot assert that either is acceptable.</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>The merchant / listing</strong></td>
+              <td><span class="chip decline">untrusted</span></td>
+              <td>Free text is fenced in tagged delimiters, scanned for injection markers, and passed to the model as data to evaluate. It is quoted as evidence, never obeyed.</td>
+            </tr>
+            <tr>
+              <td><strong>The model provider</strong></td>
+              <td><span class="chip stepup">semi-trusted</span></td>
+              <td>Trusted to return an opinion, never to decide. Its output is schema-validated, its failure escalates, and it is given no identifying field.</td>
+            </tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+
+      <h3>The mandate credential</h3>
+      <p>
+        A mandate is signed with Ed25519 over a canonical JSON payload that
+        binds all of its own fields: principal, agent, category scope, spend
+        cap, currency, expiry and a single-use nonce. Four checks run in order,
+        fail-fast, and the console shows all four including the ones that were
+        never reached.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        The binding is what makes the interesting attack fail. A
+        <em>cryptographically valid</em> signature lifted from a different
+        mandate passes every structural check; it is caught only because the
+        signature covers a payload containing that mandate's own fields, so it
+        does not verify against these. That family is in the benchmark by name.
+      </p>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ============================================================ -->
+    <section id="privacy">
+      <h2>Privacy architecture</h2>
+      <p>
+        There is no real person's data in this deployment: the principals,
+        carts and merchant ids are fictional and seeded. What follows is
+        therefore not a claim about protecting anyone's information today. It is
+        the privacy engineering the product would need before it could hold real
+        information, built and tested now rather than described as future work.
+      </p>
+
+      <div class="panel top-violet">
+        <h3 style="margin-top:0">1 &middot; Identity never reaches the model provider</h3>
+        <p>
+          An agentic-commerce system has an unusual privacy problem: to decide
+          whether a cart is legitimate, something has to read the cart, and
+          here that something is a language model at a third party. Every byte
+          of the prompt leaves the machine.
+        </p>
+        <div class="two">
+          <div>
+            <p class="lbl good">Sent</p>
+            <p>Authorized categories, spend cap, currency, cart line names,
+            quantities, prices, listing ids, and the untrusted listing text.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div>
+            <p class="lbl limit">Withheld</p>
+            <p>Principal name and id, mandate id, purchase request id, agent id,
+            mandate nonce, signature, merchant account id.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+        <p>
+          Enforced at runtime on the exact string that leaves, not by
+          convention. If a future change ever puts an identifier in the prompt,
+          the guard throws and the call is never made &mdash; and because a failed
+          engine escalates, that regression degrades into a human approval
+          rather than a leak.
+        </p>
+        <div class="two">
+          <div>
+            <p class="lbl good">What it protects</p>
+            <p>The model is handed a shopping basket with no way to know whose
+            it is.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div>
+            <p class="lbl limit">What it does not</p>
+            <p>The cart contents themselves are sent, because judging them is
+            the task. Nothing is claimed about what the provider does with them,
+            how long it retains them, or under whose jurisdiction.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="panel top-cyan">
+        <h3 style="margin-top:0">2 &middot; Four columns encrypted at rest</h3>
+        <p>
+          AES-256-GCM with a fresh 96-bit IV per value, over the principal's
+          display name, every cart line name, and the session's free text and
+          structured fields. Each ciphertext is additionally bound through the
+          AAD to its exact model, field and row id.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          That binding is the part worth pausing on. Confidentiality alone would
+          let an attacker with write access <em>move</em> a ciphertext &mdash; swap
+          two cart line names between requests, for instance. Every such move
+          decrypts perfectly under plain AES-GCM, because the bytes are
+          authentic and merely in the wrong place. Binding the AAD to
+          <code>model.field.id</code> turns a silent data-integrity attack into a
+          loud decryption error.
+        </p>
+        <div class="two">
+          <div>
+            <p class="lbl good">What it protects</p>
+            <p>An attacker with the database file and not the key: backups,
+            snapshots, open object storage, a laptop, a support export, a
+            repository someone committed the database into.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div>
+            <p class="lbl limit">What it does not</p>
+            <p>Anyone with the process, the environment or root. The key is an
+            environment variable beside the database: no KMS, no envelope
+            encryption, no rotation tooling. Amounts, categories, outcomes and
+            the audit chain are deliberately in the clear.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+        <p class="tight">
+          A thief with the file learns that a purchase happened, for how much,
+          in what category, and what STEALTH decided. Not whose it was or what
+          was in the basket. The audit chain is deliberately unencrypted: a
+          tamper-evidence log that cannot be checked without a secret is a worse
+          log.
+        </p>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="panel top-fuchsia">
+        <h3 style="margin-top:0">3 &middot; Credential material redacted server-side</h3>
+        <p>
+          The full signature, nonce, key reference and merchant account id are
+          not in the HTML, the React payload, or view-source. The server sends
+          the truncated form; revealing one is a separate request to an endpoint
+          that accepts four named fields and nothing else. A CSS blur would have
+          left the value in the page and hidden it only from the person already
+          allowed to look.
+        </p>
+        <div class="two">
+          <div>
+            <p class="lbl good">What it protects</p>
+            <p>Everything that reads the page rather than the screen:
+            view-source, devtools, scrapers, archived copies, and every
+            screenshot or recording made during a demo.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div>
+            <p class="lbl limit">What it does not</p>
+            <p>This deployment has no authentication, so anyone who can reach
+            the console can call the reveal endpoint. This is exposure
+            reduction, not access control.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+
+      <h3>Designed, and not built</h3>
+      <p>
+        Named here rather than left for a reviewer to find: authentication and
+        authorization; key management; an access log recording who revealed
+        what; and data retention and deletion, which the append-only audit chain
+        makes a genuine design problem rather than a missing function.
+      </p>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ============================================================ -->
+    <section id="audit">
+      <h2>Audit chain</h2>
+      <p>
+        Every step of every decision is appended to a SHA-256 hash chain over
+        canonical JSON. Each event carries the hash of its predecessor, so
+        editing any stored record breaks every hash after it. Verification is
+        <strong>recomputed on read</strong>, never stored, because a stored
+        "valid" flag is exactly the field an attacker would edit next.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        Mandate verification works the same way: the console never displays a
+        persisted verdict about a credential. It recomputes the four checks
+        against the live keystore and nonce ledger on every page load, so what
+        you see is the verifier's current output rather than a claim from
+        whenever the row was written.
+      </p>
+    </section>
