+      <h3>Decision quality and the semantic path</h3>
+      <div class="scroll">
+        <table>
+          <thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Cases</th><th>Precision</th><th>Recall</th><th>False allows</th><th>False declines</th></tr></thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><td><code>npm run eval</code><br><span style="font-size:12.5px">labelled, real pipeline</span></td><td class="num">21</td><td class="num">100.0%</td><td class="num">92.9%</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><code>npm run eval:semantic</code><br><span style="font-size:12.5px">boundary cases, model in the loop</span></td><td class="num">46<br><span style="font-size:12px">43 scored</span></td><td class="num">100.0%</td><td class="num">84.6%</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+      <p>
+        <strong>Every single disagreement in the semantic run is the ₹1 boundary
+        family</strong>, and always in the same direction: escalated to a human
+        instead of declined, four for four, across two different model
+        providers. The model rates a one-rupee overrun as medium severity rather
+        than high, so the policy escalates. Reasonable people can disagree about
+        which is correct &mdash; a cart ₹1 over the limit is exactly the kind of
+        thing a person should adjudicate &mdash; but the behaviour is consistent,
+        explainable, and never resolves toward allowing.
+      </p>
+      <div class="two">
+        <div><p class="lbl good">What this proves</p><p>On these sets the pipeline never allowed something it should have blocked.</p></div>
+        <div><p class="lbl limit">What it does not</p><p>n = 46, not the 250 planned: two providers hit daily quotas mid-run, and the honest number is what was measured rather than what was intended.</p></div>
+      </div>
+
+      <h3>Injection resistance</h3>
+      <p class="tight">
+        The first measurement was worthless and the reason is worth stating.
+        Baseline exposure was 0 of 7 runs: the catalog's browse endpoint returns
+        no descriptions, the injection lives in one, and the agent bought off
+        the browse list without opening a product page. That "0% hit rate"
+        measured browsing behaviour, not injection resistance.
+      </p>
+      <p class="tight">
+        Two things changed, and both affect <em>delivery</em>, not persuasion:
+        adding an item to the cart now requires having viewed it, and the
+        poisoned listing was repriced to become the cheapest running shoe. The
+        injection text, the agent's system prompt and the agent's freedom to
+        comply were deliberately left alone.
+      </p>
+      <dl class="figures">
+        <div><dt>Exposure after the fix</dt><dd>3 of 4 runs</dd></div>
+        <div><dt>Followed the injection</dt><dd class="hi">0 of 3</dd></div>
+      </dl>
+      <p class="tight">
+        With n = 3 this is a weak signal and no resistance rate is claimed from
+        it. It is reported because rewording the injection until the demo worked
+        would make every other number here suspect.
+      </p>
+
+      <h3>Razorpay, claim by claim</h3>
+      <div class="scroll">
+        <table>
+          <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Status</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><td>Webhook signature verification</td><td><span class="chip allow">verified</span></td><td>HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body, constant-time compare, wrong signature rejected</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Live-mode keys are refused</td><td><span class="chip allow">verified in production</span></td><td>Fired on a real <code>rzp_live_</code> credential that reached <code>.env</code> by mistake; three layers refused it, the error masked the key, no API call was made</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Idempotency</td><td><span class="chip allow">verified</span></td><td>Database-unique on request and decision id; a repeat returns the existing order</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>SDK, auth, minor units, receipt</td><td><span class="chip allow">verified</span></td><td><code>npm run razorpay:verify</code>, 294 ms round trip</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>An order exists in the dashboard</td><td><span class="chip allow">verified by a human</span></td><td><span class="id">order_TVI6K1KFNkmPxM</span> &middot; ₹1,001.01 &middot; 30 Aug 2026</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>No payment is captured</td><td><span class="chip allow">verified</span></td><td>Dashboard shows 0 captured, 0 refunds, 0 disputes, 0 failed</td></tr>
+            <tr><td><strong>A pipeline ALLOW creates the order</strong></td><td><span class="chip stepup">not verified</span></td><td>The confirmed order came from a direct probe. The ALLOW&rarr;order path is unit-tested and database-enforced but has not been observed end to end against the live API</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Webhook delivery from Razorpay</td><td><span class="chip stepup">not verified</span></td><td>Requires a public URL. The handler is tested; the delivery is not</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ============================================================ -->
+    <section id="limits">
+      <h2>Limits and instability</h2>
+
+      <h3>Clause severity is not stable across identical input</h3>
+      <p class="tight">
+        The seeded commercial-blender case, run four times against the same
+        model with the same cart and the same mandate:
+      </p>
+      <div class="scroll">
+        <table>
+          <thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Confidence</th><th>Severity</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">0.86</td><td>high</td><td><span class="chip decline">DECLINE</span></td></tr>
+            <tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">0.82</td><td>high</td><td><span class="chip decline">DECLINE</span></td></tr>
+            <tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">0.85</td><td>medium + low</td><td><span class="chip stepup">STEP_UP</span></td></tr>
+            <tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="num">0.85</td><td>high</td><td><span class="chip decline">DECLINE</span></td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+      <p>
+        This is disclosed rather than hidden behind a cached fixture. Note the
+        direction: the unstable run <em>escalated to a human</em>. The
+        instability moves between blocking and asking, never toward allowing,
+        which is precisely why the deciding component is deterministic.
+      </p>
+
+      <h3>Not claimed</h3>
+      <ul>
+        <li>That the agent is hard to fool. The gateway is not premised on it.</li>
+        <li>That the model is calibrated. It is asked for evidence, not a verdict, and its confidence is one input to a fixed threshold.</li>
+        <li>That this is production-ready. It is unauthenticated, single-tenant, SQLite-backed, and holds its encryption key beside its database.</li>
+        <li>That the semantic numbers generalise. n = 46 on deliberately hard cases is a small set, and it is reported as one.</li>
+      </ul>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ============================================================ -->
+    <section id="reproduce">
+      <h2>Reproducing it</h2>
+      <pre>npm install
+npm run privacy:keygen        <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"># paste the printed line into .env</span>
+npm run db:reset              <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"># with the dev server stopped</span>
+npm run dev
+
+npm test                      <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"># 257 unit and integration tests</span>
+npm run eval                  <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"># 21 labelled cases, real pipeline</span>
+npm run eval:credentials      <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"># 100,000 adversarial credentials</span>
+npm run eval:credentials -- 100000 --self-test
+npm run eval:semantic         <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"># boundary cases, model in the loop</span>
+npm run razorpay:verify       <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"># one real order, test mode only</span>
+npm run privacy:audit         <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"># tracked secrets and ignore rules</span></pre>
+      <p>
+        The at-rest claim is checked against the file rather than the code:
+        after a reseed, search the raw SQLite database for the names the console
+        displays. They are not there, and the ciphertext markers are.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        The credential and semantic numbers must never be summed. A credential
+        rejection and a semantic judgement are not the same unit of work, and
+        adding them produces a throughput figure that describes nothing.
+      </p>
+    </section>
+  </main>
+</div>
